{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And you shall make for it a grating, the work of network, of bronze.</b> For the evil inclination spreads a net to catch a person in its snare, and the net of the altar brings him out of the net of his inclination. And we find that miracles were performed through the altar: it was saved from fire — for the fire burned on it day and night and the fire did not consume it and its bronze was not melted; and it was saved from water — for the rains did not extinguish the fire of the wood of the arrangement; and it was saved from wind — for the wind did not prevail against the column of smoke. And the altar was hollow and filled with earth, or an altar of stones over which no sword had been waved. And all this is a sign for man: through the altar, a person is saved and suffers no harm from any of the four elements within him. As it is written (Psalms 66:12–13): ״בָּאנוּ בָאֵשׁ וּבַמַּיִם וַתּוֹצִיאֵנוּ לָרְוָיָה, אָבֹא בֵיתְךָ בְעוֹלוֹת״ — for just as the altar of burnt offering was saved from fire and water, so too is man saved from them through it. And likewise the altar saves him from all four deaths of the court: from stoning with stones he is saved through the altar of stones (Deuteronomy 27:5); from burning by fire he is saved through the fire of the altar that burns on it (Leviticus 6:5); from death by the sword he is saved through the altar, of which it is said (Deuteronomy 27:5), ״לֹא תָנִיף עֲלֵיהֶם בַּרְזֶל״ and so forth; from strangulation he is saved, in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ketuvot 30b): ״Even though the four deaths of the court were abolished, even so the judgment of the four deaths was not abolished — whoever is liable to strangulation or drowning in the river״ and so forth. It is found, then, that just as the altar is saved from the flood of rains, so too the sinner: ״לְשֶׁטֶף מַיִם רַבִּים אֵלָיו לֹא יַגִּיעוּ״ (Psalms 32:6)."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The Kli Yakar links the four elements (fire, water, wind, earth) to the four court-imposed deaths (burning, drowning/strangulation, stoning, sword) through the altar's physical properties and miraculous preservation; the chain of prooftexts is rendered in full."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:41.643239324Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}